\title{Temporal Provability, Persistence, and Structural Obstructions in Tree Diagrams}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{August 2, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We study a bimodal semantics separating proof accessibility from temporal accessibility. The proof modality $\Boxp$ is universal along a transitive relation $R$, while the future modality $\Fut$ is existential along a reflexive relation $T$. We prove that $\Boxp A\to\Boxp\Boxp\Fut A$ is valid under these two assumptions and factors through the ordinary principle $\Boxp A\to\Boxp\Boxp A$ and temporal reflexivity $A\to\Fut A$. Under persistence, established provability cannot be lost later; a concrete two-state model nevertheless shows that proof gain is satisfiable. Thus two readings of ``provable tomorrow but not today'' must be distinguished. We also prove that a finite tree on $n$ vertices has degree sum $2(n-1)$ and hence a vertex of degree at most one. In a simply-laced tree diagram where singleton $\rho$-dominance is equivalent to degree at least two, every tree therefore has a singleton non-dominance witness. Algorithms, applications, limitations, and precise future questions are presented.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Classical provability logic suppresses the time at which a proof is found. Mathematical practice does not: repositories grow, results depend on earlier lemmas, and an open statement may later become settled without any established theorem becoming unsettled. A temporal account needs two relations, one for proof accessibility and one for temporal succession.

A proposed interaction law is
\[
\Boxp A\longrightarrow\Boxp\Boxp\Fut A.
\]
Its nested modalities suggest a strong temporal extension. Our first result identifies its exact content: proof transitivity supplies the two boxes, and temporal reflexivity lets the current state witness future possibility. Thus this law alone cannot demonstrate strictness over an ordinary transitive provability logic.

We next distinguish proof loss from proof gain. Persistence refutes the former, while a two-state model realizes the latter. Finally, an independent tree-diagram application illustrates the same method: a global restriction is controlled by a small structural witness. Average degree below two forces a leaf, and a local degree criterion converts the leaf into failure of singleton dominance.

The results below are soundness and structural statements. Completeness, decidability, and arithmetic completeness remain open until a deductive calculus and an arithmetic clock are fixed precisely.

\section{Temporal proof frames}

\subsection{Syntax and semantics}
Fix a set $P$ of atoms. Formulas are generated from atoms, falsity $\bot$, implication, a proof modality $\Boxp$, a temporal universal modality $G$, and a temporal existential modality $\Fut$. Negation is $\neg A:=A\to\bot$.

\begin{definition}[Temporal proof frame]
A temporal proof frame is a triple $\mathcal F=(W,R,T)$, where $W$ is a nonempty set, $R\subseteq W^2$ is proof accessibility, and $T\subseteq W^2$ is temporal accessibility. For the main interaction theorem, $R$ is transitive and $T$ is reflexive.
\end{definition}

A valuation $V$ maps each atom to a subset of $W$. Boolean clauses are classical, and
\begin{align*}
\mathcal F,V,w\forces\Boxp A
&\iff \forall v\,(wRv\Rightarrow\mathcal F,V,v\forces A),\\
\mathcal F,V,w\forces GA
&\iff \forall v\,(wTv\Rightarrow\mathcal F,V,v\forces A),\\
\mathcal F,V,w\forces\Fut A
&\iff \exists v\,(wTv\land\mathcal F,V,v\forces A).
\end{align*}
The same notation applies to predicates $A:W\to\{\mathrm{false},\mathrm{true}\}$.

\begin{definition}[Persistence]
A frame is persistent if for every predicate $A$ and states $w,v$,
\[
wTv\text{ and }\Boxp A(w)\quad\Longrightarrow\quad\Boxp A(v).
\]
Equivalently, it validates $\Boxp A\to G\Boxp A$.
\end{definition}
Persistence permits new proofs but forbids loss of established ones.

\section{The temporal interaction principle}

\begin{theorem}[Temporal Interaction Theorem]
Let $(W,R,T)$ have transitive $R$ and reflexive $T$. For every predicate $A$ and $w\in W$,
\[
\Boxp A(w)\quad\Longrightarrow\quad\Boxp\Boxp\Fut A(w).
\]
Consequently every formula instance $\Boxp A\to\Boxp\Boxp\Fut A$ is valid on every such frame.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Assume $\Boxp A(w)$. Choose $v$ with $wRv$ and $u$ with $vRu$. Transitivity gives $wRu$, hence $A(u)$. Reflexivity gives $uTu$, so $u$ witnesses $\Fut A(u)$. The choices of $u$ and $v$ were arbitrary, proving the two outer universal modalities.
\end{proof}

The argument uses neither temporal transitivity, converse well-foundedness of $R$, nor a compatibility condition between the relations.

\begin{lemma}[Proof-transitivity principle]
If $R$ is transitive, then $\Boxp A\to\Boxp\Boxp A$ is valid.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
If $\Boxp A(w)$, $wRv$, and $vRu$, then $wRu$ by transitivity and hence $A(u)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Reflexive-time principle]
If $T$ is reflexive, then $A\to\Fut A$ is valid.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
If $A(w)$, then $wTw$, so $w$ itself witnesses $\Fut A(w)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Factorization Theorem]
On a frame with transitive $R$ and reflexive $T$, $\Boxp A(w)$ yields both $\Boxp\Boxp A(w)$ and $\Boxp\Boxp\Fut A(w)$. The latter is obtained by applying $A\to\Fut A$ at each innermost proof-accessible state.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The first conclusion is the proof-transitivity principle. At every state reached by two $R$-steps, it supplies $A$; the reflexive-time principle changes this to $\Fut A$ without altering the outer quantifiers.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
The scheme $\Boxp A\to\Boxp\Boxp\Fut A$ does not by itself witness a strict extension of a logic that already validates $\Boxp A\to\Boxp\Boxp A$ and is combined with reflexive temporal semantics.
\end{corollary}
This does not claim that every richer temporal calculus collapses; it says only that strictness cannot be inferred from this scheme alone.

\section{Proof loss and proof gain}

\begin{theorem}[No-Proof-Loss Theorem]
In a persistent temporal proof frame, if $today\,T\,tomorrow$, then for every predicate $A$,
\[
\neg\bigl(\Boxp A(today)\land\neg\Boxp A(tomorrow)\bigr).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Persistence carries $\Boxp A(today)$ to $\Boxp A(tomorrow)$, contradicting the second conjunct.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Proof-Gain Satisfiability Theorem]
There exist a temporal proof frame, predicate $A$, and states $today,tomorrow$ such that
\[
today\,T\,tomorrow,\qquad \neg\Boxp A(today),\qquad \Boxp A(tomorrow).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Let $W=\{0,1\}$, $today=0$, $tomorrow=1$,
\[
T=\{(0,0),(0,1),(1,1)\},\qquad R=\{(0,1)\}.
\]
The relation $R$ is transitive and $T$ is reflexive and transitive. Set $A(1)$ false. At $0$, the sole $R$-successor is $1$, where $A$ fails, so $\Boxp A(0)$ is false. At $1$ there are no $R$-successors, so $\Boxp A(1)$ is true. The selected frame is persistent as well: whenever a predicate is boxed at $0$, it holds at $1$, while boxing at $1$ is automatic.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
No calculus sound for all frames in this class can refute proof gain in general.
\end{corollary}
Thus ``provable tomorrow but not today'' has two inequivalent readings. Loss is incompatible with persistence; gain is the ordinary shape of discovery. The model concerns an ordinary predicate. A self-referential sentence about its own future status requires additional diagonal and coding analysis.

\section{Tree diagrams and the leaf obstruction}
A finite simple graph has a finite vertex set and an irreflexive symmetric adjacency relation. The degree $\deg(v)$ counts neighbors. A tree is connected and acyclic; the one-vertex graph is included.

\begin{lemma}[Tree edge count]
A finite tree with $n$ vertices has $n-1$ edges.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Induct on $n$. The one-vertex case is immediate. For $n>1$, remove an endpoint of a maximal path and its incident edge. The remainder is a tree on $n-1$ vertices, so induction gives $n-2$ remaining edges and $n-1$ originally.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Handshaking Lemma]
For every finite simple graph,
\[
\sum_{v\in V}\deg(v)=2|E|.
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Count vertex--edge incidences. Summing by vertices gives the left side; every edge has two endpoints, giving the right side.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Tree Degree-Sum Theorem]
If $G$ is a tree on $n$ vertices, then
\[
\sum_{v\in V}\deg(v)=2(n-1).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Combine the edge-count and handshaking lemmas.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Leaf Existence Theorem]
Every nonempty finite tree has a vertex $v$ with $\deg(v)\leq1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If every degree were at least two, then $2n\leq\sum_v\deg(v)=2(n-1)$, an impossibility. For $n\geq2$, connectedness upgrades the conclusion to degree exactly one.
\end{proof}

Now let a simply-laced tree diagram have vertex set $I$. Write $\alpha_v$ for the simple root indexed by $v$, $\rho$ for the half-sum of positive roots, and $\beta_I$ for the correction associated with the full diagram. For a singleton marking define
\[
\lambda_{\{v\},I}=2\rho-\beta_I-\alpha_v.
\]

\begin{definition}[Singleton $\rho$-dominance criterion]
In the simply-laced setting considered here, the singleton correction at $v$ is $\rho$-dominant exactly when
\[
\deg(v)\geq2.
\]
Equivalently, $\lambda_{\{v\},I}$ is $\rho$-dominant if and only if $\deg(v)\geq2$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Leaf Obstruction Theorem]
Every nonempty simply-laced tree diagram contains a vertex $v$ for which
\[
\lambda_{\{v\},I}=2\rho-\beta_I-\alpha_v
\]
is not $\rho$-dominant.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Choose $v$ with $\deg(v)\leq1$ by the Leaf Existence Theorem. Singleton dominance requires $\deg(v)\geq2$, so it fails at $v$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
Every nonempty tree component excludes at least one singleton marking; indeed, every leaf is excluded.
\end{corollary}
A cycle can have every degree equal to two, so acyclicity is the source of this obstruction.

\section{Finite algorithms}
For an $N$-state frame, store $R$ and $T$ as adjacency lists or Boolean matrices. Given a Boolean vector $A$, compute
\begin{align*}
(\Boxp A)[w]&=\bigwedge_{v:R[w,v]}A[v],\\
(GA)[w]&=\bigwedge_{v:T[w,v]}A[v],\\
(\Fut A)[w]&=\bigvee_{v:T[w,v]}A[v].
\end{align*}
A bottom-up evaluation of a formula with $m$ subformulas costs $O(mN^2)$ with dense matrices and $O(m(N+|R|+|T|))$ with adjacency lists. Exhausting the $2^N$ valuations tests a one-atom scheme on a fixed small frame. Alternatively, checking transitivity of $R$ in $O(N^3)$ and reflexivity of $T$ in $O(N)$ lets the Temporal Interaction Theorem certify every valuation at once.

Proof loss and gain are detected by computing $\Boxp A$ at designated states. Persistence for a fixed valuation is checked along every temporal edge. Exhaustive valuation search is suitable for small countermodels but does not itself establish completeness of a calculus.

For a graph with $n$ vertices and $m$ edges, depth-first search checks connectedness and acyclicity in $O(n+m)$ time while degrees are accumulated. If the graph is a tree, the degree identity provides a consistency check. A linear scan finds leaves, which are immediate non-dominance witnesses under the singleton criterion. The full graph procedure is linear.

\section{Applications and discussion}
A versioned theorem repository naturally gives temporal states. Persistence expresses the ideal that accepted results remain available after an update; proof gain represents newly added lemmas. This distinction supports regression testing: loss is an anomaly, gain is expected.

In dependency-aware search, states record available lemmas, temporal edges encode search expansion, and proof edges encode deductive accessibility. The factorization theorem warns that an apparently temporal axiom may contribute no new pruning power. More informative principles must use genuinely later states or stronger interaction between the relations.

For proof provenance, time stamps identify when a result becomes available. A strict-future modality would be especially informative: unlike reflexive $\Fut$, it would not validate $A\to\Fut A$ automatically.

In diagram classification, degree computation is inexpensive preprocessing. Every leaf can be rejected as a singleton marking before more expensive weight calculations.

There are important limitations. No completeness theorem can be stated without a precise calculus and frame class. Decidability requires, for example, a finite-model property with an effective bound. Arithmetic requires a clock: an increasing recursively axiomatized sequence $(PA_t)$ and a precise interpretation $\Boxp_t A=\operatorname{Prov}_{PA_t}(\ulcorner A\urcorner)$. Different clocks may validate different laws. Finally, the graph lemmas are universal, but their dominance consequence depends on the stated simply-laced singleton criterion.

\section{Future work}
A concrete program consists of the following problems.
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
\item Define a Hilbert calculus containing ordinary provability logic, temporal $S4$, persistence $\Boxp A\to G\Boxp A$, and explicit interactions. Test finite-frame completeness.
\item Establish or refute the proposed countermodel bound $2^{2s(A)}$, where $s(A)$ counts subformulas, using filtration and bounded search.
\item If such a bound and completeness hold, obtain decidability through terminating finite-frame model checking.
\item Characterize frame classes admitting proof gain; the two-state model refutes any universal prohibition on the present class.
\item Fix an increasing sequence $(PA_t)$ and compare a specified recursively enumerable modal calculus with formulas valid under every arithmetic substitution.
\item Study a strict-future operator, for which the reflexive factorization no longer applies automatically.
\item Count forbidden singleton markings in forests using leaves, branching, and degree distributions.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}
The interaction $\Boxp A\to\Boxp\Boxp\Fut A$ is valid for a simple reason: transitivity supplies nested proof necessity and reflexivity makes the present a possible future. It therefore does not alone demonstrate a strict temporal extension. Persistence rules out proof loss but not discovery; a two-state model realizes proof gain.

The tree application offers a complementary structural lesson. Since a tree has degree sum $2(n-1)$, it has a leaf. If singleton dominance requires degree at least two, every tree contains a forbidden singleton. In both settings, a local witness settles a global-looking question and sharply separates established results from the stronger conjectures that remain.

\end{document}

